(*
$Id: action_schema.exp,v 1.31 2019/03/29 20:48:36 tom Exp $
ISO 10303 TC184/SC4/WG12 N10230
*)

SCHEMA action_schema '{iso standard 10303 part(41) version(8) object(1) action_schema(1)}';

(*"action_schema"

@version = '8'
@number = '10230'
@number.supersedes = '8369'
@remark.tag.encoding = 'metanorma'

== General

The subject of the *action_schema* is the description of actions,
the reasons for these actions, and the status of these actions.

EXAMPLE: Reasons for action include evolving user requirements,
manufacturing problems and difficulties that arise when a product
is in use.

== Fundamental concepts and assumptions

Action information can be attached to any aspect of product data.

== EXPRESS-G diagram of the action_schema

image::action_schemaexpg1.xml[]

image::action_schemaexpg2.xml[]

*)

  REFERENCE FROM basic_attribute_schema (	-- ISO 10303-41
    description_attribute,
    description_attribute_select,
    get_description_value,
    get_id_value,
    get_name_value,
    get_role,
    id_attribute,
    id_attribute_select,
    name_attribute,
    name_attribute_select,
    object_role,
    role_select,
    role_association);


  REFERENCE FROM support_resource_schema ( -- ISO 10303-41
    bag_to_set,
    identifier,
    label,
    text);



  TYPE as_description_attribute_select = SELECT BASED_ON description_attribute_select WITH (
    action_request_solution);
  END_TYPE;



  TYPE as_id_attribute_select = SELECT BASED_ON id_attribute_select WITH (
    action);
  END_TYPE;



  TYPE as_name_attribute_select = SELECT BASED_ON name_attribute_select WITH (
    action_request_solution);
  END_TYPE;



  TYPE as_role_select = SELECT BASED_ON role_select WITH (
    directed_action_assignment);
  END_TYPE;



  TYPE supported_item = SELECT (action_directive, action, action_method);
  END_TYPE;

(*"supported_item"
	The supported_item allows for the designation of an
	express_ref:[action_schema.action_directive],
	an express_ref:[action_schema.action],
	or an express_ref:[action_schema.action_method].

NOTE: This specifies the use of an
	express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource].

*)


  ENTITY action;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    chosen_method : action_method;
  DERIVE
    id : identifier := get_id_value(SELF);
  WHERE
    WR1: SIZEOF(USEDIN(SELF, 'BASIC_ATTRIBUTE_SCHEMA.' + 'ID_ATTRIBUTE.IDENTIFIED_ITEM')) <= 1;
  END_ENTITY;

(*"action"

An *action* is the identification of the occurrence of an activity and a description of its result.

An *action* identifies an activity that has taken place, is taking place, or is expected to take place in the future.

An action has a definition that is specified by an
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method].

NOTE: In particular application domains, terms such as task, process, activity, operation, and event may be synonyms for *action*.

[example]
====
Change, distilling, design, a process to drill a hole, and a task such as training someone are examples of actions.
====

*)

(*"action.name"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action* is known.
*)

(*"action.description"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)

(*"action.chosen_method"
the express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that specifies the procedure selected to carry out the *action* and a description of the results of that *action*.
*)

(*"action.id"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.identifier]
that distinguishes the *action*.

NOTE: This attribute is an enhancement to the definition of *action* using a method that is upwardly compatible
with ISO 10303-41:1994.

NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

NOTE: The context in which id is used as a discriminating characteristic can be identified in an annotated
Express schema that uses or specializes this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between
partners sharing this information.

*)

(*"action.wr:WR1"

Each *action* shall be the
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.id_attribute.identified_item]
in at most one
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.id_attribute].

NOTE: The express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.id_attribute]
data type is defined in
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema]
of this part of ISO 10303.

NOTE: A template for constraining the population of the entity data types defined in the
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema]
is described in annex E.

*)
(*"action.ip:IP1"

This is a dummy ip inserted for prototyping.

note that the e.g., arm.xml uses a special encoding of a WR name to indicate an IP.
We propose to define a type for consistency.*)



  ENTITY action_directive;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    analysis : text;
    comment : text;
    requests : SET [1:?] OF versioned_action_request;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_directive"
An *action_directive* is an authoritative instrument that provides directions to achieve the specified results.

[example]
====
ISO Directives Part 3 provides guidance for the development of standards documents within ISO.
====

*)


(*"action_directive.analysis"

an informal description of the results of the analysis that was carried out on the elements of the requests set.
The request sets are the
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]s
referenced in the requests attribute.

NOTE: The reason that different requests are satisfied by the *action_directive* could be recorded in this attribute.

*)


(*"action_directive.comment"
an informal description of any other pertinent information.
*)


(*"action_directive.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_directive*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_directive.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_directive* is known.
*)


(*"action_directive.requests"
a set of
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
entity data types that defines the expected results.
*)



  ENTITY action_method;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    consequence : text;
    purpose : text;
  END_ENTITY;



(*"action_method"
An *action_method* is the definition of an activity.
This definition includes the activity’s objectives and effects.

NOTE: This definition may be the basis for actions or the solution for action requests.


[example]
====

For the
express_ref:[action_schema.action]
whose name attribute is 'serve dinner', the name attribute of related instance of *action_method* could
be 'cook by recipe' or 'purchase takeout food'.
====

*)


(*"action_method.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_method* is known.
*)


(*"action_method.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_method*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_method.consequence"
an informal description of the effects of the *action_method*.
*)


(*"action_method.purpose"
an informal description of the objectives of the *action_method*.
*)




  ENTITY action_method_relationship;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    relating_method : action_method;
    related_method : action_method;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_method_relationship"
An *action_method_relationship* is a relationship between two instances of the entity data type
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
and provides an identification and description of this relationship.

NOTE: The role of *action_method_relationship* can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or
specialize this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.


NOTE: This entity, together with the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
entity, is based on the relationship template that is described in annex E.3.


NOTE: This entity may be used to define a procedural relationship among constituent activities.

*)


(*"action_method_relationship.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_method_relationship* is known.
*)


(*"action_method_relationship.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_method_relationship*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_method_relationship.relating_method"

one of the instances of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that is a part of the relationship.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)


(*"action_method_relationship.related_method"

the other instance of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that is a part of the relationship.
If one element of the relationship is dependent upon the other this attribute shall be the dependent one.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)



  ENTITY action_relationship;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    relating_action : action;
    related_action : action;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_relationship"
An *action_relationship* is a relationship between two instances of the entity data type
express_ref:[action_schema.action]
and provides an identification and description of this relationship.

NOTE: The role of *action_relationship* can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or
specialize this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)


(*"action_relationship.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_relationship* is known.
*)


(*"action_relationship.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_relationship*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_relationship.relating_action"

one of the instances of
express_ref:[action_schema.action]
that is a part of the relationship.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)


(*"action_relationship.related_action"

the other instance of
express_ref:[action_schema.action]
that is a part of the relationship.
If one element of the relationship is dependent upon the other this attribute shall be the dependent one.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)



  ENTITY action_request_solution;
    method : action_method;
    request : versioned_action_request;
  DERIVE
    description : text := get_description_value(SELF);
    name : label := get_name_value(SELF);
  WHERE
    WR1: SIZEOF(USEDIN(SELF, 'BASIC_ATTRIBUTE_SCHEMA.' + 'DESCRIPTION_ATTRIBUTE.DESCRIBED_ITEM')) <= 1;
    WR2: SIZEOF(USEDIN(SELF, 'BASIC_ATTRIBUTE_SCHEMA.' + 'NAME_ATTRIBUTE.NAMED_ITEM')) <= 1;
  END_ENTITY;



(*"action_request_solution"
An *action_request_solution* is an association between a
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
and an
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that is a potential solution for the request.
*)


(*"action_request_solution.method"
the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that is a potential solution.
*)


(*"action_request_solution.request"
the
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
for which a solution is specified.
*)


(*"action_request_solution.description"

the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_request_solution*.


NOTE: This attribute is an upwardly compatible addition to *action_request_solution* as specified in ISO 10303-41:1994.

*)


(*"action_request_solution.name"

the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_request_solution* is known.


NOTE: This attribute is an upwardly compatible addition to *action_request_solution* as specified in ISO 10303-41:1994.

*)

(*"action_request_solution.wr:WR1"

Each *action_request_solution* shall be the
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.description_attribute.described_item]
in at most one
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.description_attribute].


NOTE: The
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.description_attribute]
data type is defined in
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema]
of this part of ISO 10303.

*)

(*"action_request_solution.wr:WR2"

Each *action_request_solution* shall be the
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.name_attribute.named_item]
in at most one
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.name_attribute].


NOTE: The
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.name_attribute]
data type is defined in
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema]
of this part of ISO 10303.


NOTE: A template for constraining the population of the entity data types defined in the
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema]
is described in annex E.

*)



  ENTITY action_request_status;
    status : label;
    assigned_request : versioned_action_request;
  END_ENTITY;

(*"action_request_status"
An *action_request_status* is the association of a status with a
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request].
*)


(*"action_request_status.assigned_request"
the
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
to which the *status* applies.
*)


(*"action_request_status.status"

the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
that provides a user interpretable designation for the level of completion of the action.

NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize this
entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)



  ENTITY action_resource;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    usage : SET [1:?] OF supported_item;
    kind : action_resource_type;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_resource"
An *action_resource* is a thing that is identified as being needed to carry out an action.
*)


(*"action_resource.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_resource*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_resource.kind"
the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource_type]
that specifies the sort of *action_resource* that is being used.
*)


(*"action_resource.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_resource* is known.
*)


(*"action_resource.usage"
a set of
express_ref:[action_schema.supported_item]s
for which the *action_resource* is used.
*)



  ENTITY action_resource_relationship;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    relating_resource : action_resource;
    related_resource : action_resource;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_resource_relationship"
An *action_resource_relationship* is a relationship between two instances of the entity data type
express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource]
and provides an identification and description of this relationship.

NOTE: The role of *action_resource_relationship* can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use
or specialize this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.


NOTE: This entity, together with the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource]
entity, is based on the relationship template that is described in annex E.3.

*)


(*"action_resource_relationship.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *action_resource_relationship*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_resource_relationship.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_resource_relationship* is known.
*)


(*"action_resource_relationship.related_resource"

the other instance of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource]
that is a part of the relationship.
If one element of the relationship is dependent upon the other this attribute shall be the dependent one.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)


(*"action_resource_relationship.relating_resource"

one of the instances of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource]
that is a part of the relationship.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)



  ENTITY action_resource_type;
    name : label;
  END_ENTITY;



(*"action_resource_type"
An *action_resource_type* is the identification of the kind of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_resource]
needed to carry out an action.

[example]
====
This entity may be used to specify the kind of tool needed to perform a process operation.
====

*)


(*"action_resource_type.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *action_resource_type* is known.
*)




  ENTITY action_status;
    status : label;
    assigned_action : executed_action;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_status"
An *action_status* is the association of a status with an
express_ref:[action_schema.executed_action].

NOTE: Information about the date and time may be associated with the *action_status* through the use of
express_ref:[management_resources_schema.date_assignment],
express_ref:[management_resources_schema.date_and_time_assignment],
or
express_ref:[management_resources_schema.time_assignment].
</note>
*)


(*"action_status.assigned_action"
the
express_ref:[action_schema.executed_action]
to which the status applies.
*)


(*"action_status.status"

the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
that provides a user interpretable designation for the level of completion of the action.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.


[example]
====
Terms such as 'pending', 'completed', or 'scheduled' are examples of status.
====

*)



  ENTITY directed_action
    SUBTYPE OF (executed_action);
    directive : action_directive;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"directed_action"
A *directed_action* is a type of
express_ref:[action_schema.executed_action]
that is governed by an
express_ref:[action_schema.action_directive].

[example]
====
A *directed_action* could be the inspection of a building as directed by city officials according to the
city building codes for earthquake safety.
The action is the inspection of the building.
The directive is issued by city officials guided by the city building codes.
In an application protocol, the building authority may be associated with an
express_ref:[management_resources_schema.organization_assignment].
The building codes may be associated with a
express_ref:[management_resources_schema.document_reference].
====

*)


(*"directed_action.directive"
the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_directive]
that governs the *directed_action*.
*)




  ENTITY directed_action_assignment
  	ABSTRACT SUPERTYPE ;
  	assigned_directed_action : directed_action;
    DERIVE
  	role : object_role := get_role(SELF);
    WHERE
  	WR1: SIZEOF(USEDIN(SELF, 'BASIC_ATTRIBUTE_SCHEMA.' + 'ROLE_ASSOCIATION.ITEM_WITH_ROLE')) <= 1;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"directed_action_assignment"
A *directed_action_assignment*
is an association of a
express_ref:[action_schema.directed_action]
with product data.
*)


(*"directed_action_assignment.assigned_directed_action"
the instance of the  *directed_action* entity data that is to be associated with product data.
*)


(*"directed_action_assignment.role"
the *object_role* that specifies the purpose of the association of the *directed_action_assignment* with product data.
*)

(*"directed_action_assignment.wr:WR1"

Each *directed_action_assignment* shall be
*item_with_role*
in at most one
express_ref:[basic_attribute_schema.role_association].

*)




  ENTITY executed_action
    SUBTYPE OF (action);
  END_ENTITY;


(*"executed_action"
An *executed_action* is a type of
express_ref:[action_schema.action]
that is completed, partially completed, or just identified.
It may but need not have status information associated with it.

NOTE: The role of *executed_action* can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

NOTE: Status information is associated to *executed_action* through
express_ref:[action_schema.action_status].


[example]
====
An *executed_action* could be to 'paint the office' with a status of 'scheduled'.
The action is 'paint the office'.
The status further qualifies the action as 'planned', 'scheduled', or 'completed'.
====

*)



  ENTITY versioned_action_request;
    id : identifier;
    version : OPTIONAL label;
    purpose : text;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
  END_ENTITY;


(*"versioned_action_request"
A *versioned_action_request* is a specification of a desired result.

NOTE: The desired result being identified and described may be obtained through one of more
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]s.

*)


(*"versioned_action_request.id"

the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.identifier]
that distinguishes the *versioned_action_request*.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.


NOTE: The context in which id is used as a discriminating characteristic can be identified in an annotated
Express schema that uses or specializes this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between
partners sharing this information.

*)


(*"versioned_action_request.version"
the identification of the version of the *versioned_action_request*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"versioned_action_request.purpose"
an informal description of the reason for the *versioned_action_request*.
*)


(*"versioned_action_request.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *versioned_action_request*.
The value of the attribute need not be specified.
*)



  ENTITY versioned_action_request_relationship;
    id : identifier;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    relating_versioned_action_request : versioned_action_request;
    related_versioned_action_request : versioned_action_request;
  END_ENTITY;



(*"versioned_action_request_relationship"
A *versioned_action_request_relationship* is a relationship between two
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
objects.

[example]
Two express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request] objects may  be related if
they address similar problems.

[example]
A express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request] may be a version of a work request.
It might be related to a different version of the work request using a *versioned_action_request_relationship*.

*)


(*"versioned_action_request_relationship.id"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.identifier]
that distinguishes the *versioned_action_request_relationship*.
*)


(*"versioned_action_request_relationship.name"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label]
by which the *versioned_action_request_relationship* is known.
*)


(*"versioned_action_request_relationship.description"
the
express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text]
that characterizes the *versioned_action_request_relationship*.
The value of this attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"versioned_action_request_relationship.relating_versioned_action_request"

one of the instances of
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
that is a part of the relationship.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)


(*"versioned_action_request_relationship.related_versioned_action_request"

the other instance of
express_ref:[action_schema.versioned_action_request]
that is a part of the relationship.
If one element of the relationship is dependent upon the other, this attribute shall be the dependent one.


NOTE: The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize
this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.

*)



  ENTITY action_directive_relationship;
  	name			: label;
  	description 	: OPTIONAL STRING;
  	relating 		: action_directive;
  	related 		: action_directive;
  	relation_type 	: STRING;
   WHERE
  	WR1 : acyclic_action_directive_relationship(SELF, [related], 'ACTION_SCHEMA.ACTION_DIRECTIVE_RELATIONSHIP');
  END_ENTITY;


(*"action_directive_relationship"
An *action_directive_relationship* is a relationship between two
express_ref:[action_schema.action_directive]
objects.
*)


(*"action_directive_relationship.name"
shall be unique within a population of *action_directive_relationship*.
*)


(*"action_directive_relationship.description"
the text or the set of texts that provide further information about the *action_directive_relationship*.
The value of this attribute need not be specified.
*)


(*"action_directive_relationship.related"
the other object of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_directive]
that is part of the relationship.
*)


(*"action_directive_relationship.relating"
one object of
express_ref:[action_schema.action_directive]
that is part of the relationship.
*)


(*"action_directive_relationship.relation_type"
the meaning of the relationship.
*)


(*"action_directive_relationship.wr:WR1"
A *action_directive_relationship* shall not participate in its own definition.
*)

  FUNCTION acyclic_action_method_relationship (relation : action_method_relationship; relatives : SET [1:?] OF action_method; specific_relation : STRING):BOOLEAN;
    LOCAL
      x : SET OF action_method_relationship;
    END_LOCAL;

    IF relation.relating_method IN relatives THEN
      RETURN (FALSE);
    END_IF;
    x := QUERY(am <* bag_to_set(USEDIN(relation.relating_method, 'ACTION_SCHEMA.' + 'ACTION_METHOD_RELATIONSHIP.' + 'RELATED')) | specific_relation IN TYPEOF(am));
    REPEAT i := 1 TO HIINDEX(x);
      IF NOT acyclic_action_method_relationship(x[i], relatives + relation.relating_method, specific_relation) THEN
        RETURN (FALSE);
      END_IF;
    END_REPEAT;
    RETURN (TRUE);
  END_FUNCTION;

(*"acyclic_action_method_relationship"
The *acyclic_action_method_relationship* function determines whether the graph of instances of the entity data type
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that contains relation as one of its links contains a cycle.
This function may be used to evaluate either an
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method_relationship]
or any of its subtypes.

The function returns TRUE if no cycle has been detected. Otherwise it returns FALSE.

NOTE: The algorithm of the function is explained in annex E.2.

NOTE: This function is not used in this schema. It is defined here because other ISO 10303 integrated resources
and application protocols that use the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method_relationship]
entity include rules that use this function.

*)


(*"acyclic_action_method_relationship.relation"
(input) the candidate
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method_relationship]
to be checked.
*)

(*"acyclic_action_method_relationship.relatives"
(input) the set of instances of the entity data type
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method]
that the function is searching for in the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method_relationship.relating_method]
parameter of the relation argument.
*)

(*"acyclic_action_method_relationship.specific_relation"
(input) the fully qualified name of a subtype of the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_method_relationship]
entity.
*)


  FUNCTION acyclic_action_relationship (relation : action_relationship; relatives : SET [1:?] OF action; specific_relation : STRING):BOOLEAN;
    LOCAL
      x : SET OF action_relationship;
    END_LOCAL;

    IF relation.relating_action IN relatives THEN
      RETURN (FALSE);
    END_IF;
    x := QUERY(actn <* bag_to_set(USEDIN(relation.relating_action, 'ACTION_SCHEMA.' + 'ACTION_RELATIONSHIP.' + 'RELATED_ACTION')) | specific_relation IN TYPEOF(actn));
    REPEAT i := 1 TO HIINDEX(x);
      IF NOT acyclic_action_relationship(x[i], relatives + relation.relating_action, specific_relation) THEN
        RETURN (FALSE);
      END_IF;
    END_REPEAT;
    RETURN (TRUE);
  END_FUNCTION;


(*"acyclic_action_relationship"
The *acyclic_action_relationship* function determines whether the graph of instances of the entity data type
express_ref:[action_schema.action]
that contains relation as one of its links contains a cycle.
This function may be used to evaluate either an
express_ref:[action_schema.action_relationship]
or any of its subtypes.

The function returns TRUE if no cycle has been detected. Otherwise it returns FALSE.

NOTE: The algorithm of the function is explained in annex E.2.

NOTE: This function is not used in this schema.
It is defined here because other ISO 10303 integrated resources and application protocols that use the
express_ref:[action_schema.action_relationship]
entity include rules that use this function.

*)

(*"acyclic_action_relationship.relation"
(input) the candidate
express_ref:[action_schema.action_relationship]
to be checked.
